sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch10-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "To call something complex is to say that our methods of perception and analysis have failed to find a short description of the features of it that we are interested in; complexity therefore depends on which features one chooses to capture.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Complexity means our perception and analysis fail to find a short description";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 10, “Processes of Perception and Analysis”; section “Defining Complexity” (from p. 557), p. 557. Scope: the definition of complexity"
}
